<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-finding-the-derivative-graphically" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Finding the Derivative Graphically</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>4.0 Calculus</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>4.2 Differentiation I</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>
                        Sketch the derivative of a function from its graph and identify where
                        the derivative is positive, negative, or zero
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>11</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>40 minutes</p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>12 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    Discover that plotting the slopes of tangent lines at several points of
                    <m>f</m> produces the graph of <m>f'</m>, and that the shape of <m>f</m>
                    (increasing/decreasing/flat) determines the sign of <m>f'</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Distribute printed copies of <xref ref="fig-activity-graphical-blank"/> if
                    possible. Ask learners to lay a ruler so it just touches/kisses the curve at
                    each marked point without crossing it.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="activity-reading-slopes">
        <title>Reading Slopes</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The graph below shows <m>f(x) = x^2</m>. Five points on the curve
                are marked at <m>x = -2, -1, 0, 1, 2</m>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-activity-graphical-blank">
                <caption>
                    Graph of <m>f(x) = x^2</m> with five marked points.
                    Draw a tangent line at each marked point.
                </caption>
                <image>
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.05]
                            % Fine grid
                            \draw[gray!25, step=0.25] (-2.6,-0.4) grid (2.6,4.4);
                            % Bold grid
                            \draw[gray!55, step=1] (-2.6,-0.4) grid (2.6,4.4);
                            % Axes
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-2.7,0) -- (2.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-0.4) -- (0,4.6) node[above] {$y$};
                            % Tick labels
                            \foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2}
                                \draw (\x,0.08) -- (\x,-0.08) node[below] {\small $\x$};
                            \foreach \y in {1,2,3,4}
                                \draw (0.08,\y) -- (-0.08,\y) node[left] {\small $\y$};
                            % Curve
                            \draw[blue, very thick, domain=-2.05:2.05, smooth, samples=80]
                                plot (\x, {\x*\x});
                            \node[blue] at (1.6,3.8) {$f(x)$};
                            % Marked points
                            \foreach \x/\y in {-2/4, -1/1, 0/0, 2/4}
                                \filldraw[red] (\x,\y) circle (2pt);
                            \filldraw[orange] (1,1) circle (2pt);
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        We want to figure out the slope of <m>f(x)</m> at the orange dot <m>x = 1</m>.
                        <ol marker="(i)">
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    Draw a straight line that just <q>kisses</q> the graph <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x = 1</m>.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    Mark another point on your line, and determine its <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> coordinate.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    Calculate the gradient of your line using the <xref ref="insight-aroc-formula"/> formula.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            The tangent at <m>x = 1</m> has slope <m>2</m>.
                            For example, the tangent line <m>y = 2x - 1</m> passes through <m>(1, 1)</m>
                            and also through <m>(2, 3)</m>: rise <m>= 2</m>, run <m>= 1</m>, so gradient <m>= 2</m>.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            The diagram below shows all five tangent lines drawn — ask learners to check their drawn lines match.
                        </p>
                        <figure xml:id="fig-activity-graphical-solved">
                            <caption>
                                Tangent lines at <m>x = -2, -1, 0, 1, 2</m> with slopes labelled.
                            </caption>
                            <image width="75%">
                                <latex-image>
                                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.05]
                                        \draw[gray!25, step=0.25] (-2.6,-0.4) grid (2.6,4.4);
                                        \draw[gray!55, step=1] (-2.6,-0.4) grid (2.6,4.4);
                                        \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-2.7,0) -- (2.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                        \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-0.4) -- (0,4.6) node[above] {$y$};
                                        \foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2}
                                            \draw (\x,0.08) -- (\x,-0.08) node[below] {\small $\x$};
                                        \foreach \y in {1,2,3,4}
                                            \draw (0.08,\y) -- (-0.08,\y) node[left] {\small $\y$};
                                        \draw[blue, very thick, domain=-2.05:2.05, smooth, samples=80]
                                            plot (\x, {\x*\x});
                                        \draw[red!70, thick, domain=-2.35:-1.65]
                                            plot (\x, {-4*\x - 4});
                                        \filldraw[red] (-2,4) circle (3.5pt);
                                        \node[red, right] at (-1.95,4.3) {\small $m=-4$};
                                        \draw[red!70, thick, domain=-1.6:-0.4]
                                            plot (\x, {-2*\x - 1});
                                        \filldraw[red] (-1,1) circle (3.5pt);
                                        \node[red, above left] at (-1.6,1.5) {\small $m=-2$};
                                        \draw[red!70, thick, domain=-0.7:0.7]
                                            plot (\x, {0});
                                        \filldraw[red] (0,0) circle (3.5pt);
                                        \node[red, below] at (0,-0.25) {\small $m=0$};
                                        \draw[orange!80, thick, domain=0.4:1.6]
                                            plot (\x, {2*\x - 1});
                                        \filldraw[orange] (1,1) circle (3.5pt);
                                        \node[orange!80, above right] at (1.55,1.5) {\small $m=2$};
                                        \draw[red!70, thick, domain=1.65:2.35]
                                            plot (\x, {4*\x - 4});
                                        \filldraw[red] (2,4) circle (3.5pt);
                                        \node[red, left] at (1.95,4.3) {\small $m=4$};
                                    \end{tikzpicture}
                                </latex-image>
                            </image>
                        </figure>
                    </solution>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Repeat (a) for the red points, and fill in the table:
                    </p>
                    <table>
                        <title>Estimated tangent slopes</title>
                        <tabular halign="center">
                            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                                <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                                <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
                                <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                                <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                                <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                                <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                            </row>
                            <row>
                                <cell>Estimated slope</cell>
                                <cell></cell>
                                <cell></cell>
                                <cell></cell>
                                <cell></cell>
                                <cell></cell>
                            </row>
                        </tabular>
                    </table>
                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <table>
                            <title>Tangent slopes (exact)</title>
                            <tabular halign="center">
                                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                                </row>
                                <row>
                                    <cell>Slope <m>f'(x)</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>-4</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>4</m></cell>
                                </row>
                            </tabular>
                        </table>
                    </solution>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        What do you think the pattern is?
                    </p>
                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            Each slope equals <m>2x</m>: the slope at <m>x = -2</m> is <m>-4</m>,
                            at <m>x = -1</m> is <m>-2</m>, and so on. So <m>f'(x) = 2x</m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Plot the points <m>(x, \text{slope})</m> on a new set of axes.
                        What shape do they form?
                    </p>
                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            The five points <m>(-2,-4),\,(-1,-2),\,(0,0),\,(1,2),\,(2,4)</m>
                            lie on a straight line through the origin — a linear graph.
                            The diagrams below show <m>f</m> alongside the line of slopes <m>f'(x) = 2x</m>.
                        </p>
                        <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="auto">
                            <figure xml:id="fig-activity-f">
                                <caption><m>f(x) = x^2</m></caption>
                                <image>
                                    <latex-image>
                                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
                                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-2.6,0) -- (2.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-0.4) -- (0,4.6) node[above] {$y$};
                                            \foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2}
                                                \draw (\x,0.08) -- (\x,-0.08) node[below] {\small $\x$};
                                            \foreach \y in {1,2,3,4}
                                                \draw (0.08,\y) -- (-0.08,\y) node[left] {\small $\y$};
                                            \draw[blue, very thick, domain=-2.05:2.05, smooth, samples=70]
                                                plot (\x, {\x*\x});
                                            \node[blue] at (1.6,3.8) {\small $f(x)$};
                                            \draw[orange, thick] (-0.6,0) -- (0.6,0);
                                            \node[orange, right] at (0.65,0.3) {\scriptsize $f'=0$};
                                            \draw[dashed, gray!70] (0,-0.4) -- (0,4.6);
                                            \node at (-1.5,2.5) {\small $f'\!&lt;\!0$};
                                            \node at (1.5,2.5) {\small $f'\!&gt;\!0$};
                                        \end{tikzpicture}
                                    </latex-image>
                                </image>
                            </figure>
                            <figure xml:id="fig-activity-fprime">
                                <caption><m>f'(x) = 2x</m> (the slopes plotted)</caption>
                                <image>
                                    <latex-image>
                                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
                                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-2.6,0) -- (2.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-4.8) -- (0,4.8) node[above] {$y$};
                                            \foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2}
                                                \draw (\x,0.08) -- (\x,-0.08) node[below] {\small $\x$};
                                            \foreach \y in {-4,-2,2,4}
                                                \draw (0.08,\y) -- (-0.08,\y) node[left] {\small $\y$};
                                            \draw[red!80, very thick, domain=-2.2:2.2]
                                                plot (\x, {2*\x});
                                            \node[red!80] at (1.5,3.5) {\small $f'(x)$};
                                            \filldraw[red!80] (0,0) circle (3.5pt);
                                            \node[red!80, above right] at (0.05,0.1) {\scriptsize $f'(0)=0$};
                                            \draw[dashed, gray!70] (0,-4.8) -- (0,4.8);
                                            \node at (-1.4,-1) {\small $f'\!&lt;\!0$};
                                            \node at (1.4,1) {\small $f'\!&gt;\!0$};
                                        \end{tikzpicture}
                                    </latex-image>
                                </image>
                            </figure>
                        </sidebyside>
                    </solution>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        At which value of <m>x</m> is the tangent horizontal?
                        What is special about <m>f</m> at that point?
                    </p>
                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            At <m>x = 0</m> the tangent is horizontal (slope <m>0</m>).
                            This is the vertex of the parabola — its minimum.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Where is <m>f</m> increasing? Where is the estimated slope positive?
                        Where is <m>f</m> decreasing? Where is the slope negative?
                        What do you notice?
                    </p>
                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            <m>f</m> is decreasing for <m>x \lt 0</m> — the slopes there are negative.
                            <m>f</m> is increasing for <m>x \gt 0</m> — the slopes are positive.
                            The sign of the slope always matches the direction of the curve.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <exploration>
        <title>Finding the Derivative Graphically</title>
        <p>
            Use the interactive graph below to check your tangent estimates.
        </p>
        <p>
            Drag the red point along the curve to see the tangent line and slope at any position.
        </p>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-derivative-graphical" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="1:1" source="jsxgraph/derivative-graphical.js">
            <slate xml:id="derivative-graphical" surface="jsxboard" aspect="1:1"/>
            <instructions>
                <p>Drag the red point along the curve to read off the slope of the tangent at each marked point.</p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </exploration>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>8 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    Formalise the geometric meaning of the derivative: the slope of the tangent
                    line. Connect the shape of <m>f</m> to the sign of <m>f'</m>, and introduce
                    the method of sketching <m>f'</m> from <m>f</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <insight>
        <title>Key Ideas</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The <term>tangent line</term> at a point on a curve is the line that
                        best approximates the curve there. Its slope is the derivative at that point.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Where <m>f</m> is <em>increasing</em>, <m>f'(x) \gt 0</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Where <m>f</m> is <em>decreasing</em>, <m>f'(x) \lt 0</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        At a <em>local maximum or minimum</em> of <m>f</m>, the tangent is
                        horizontal: <m>f'(x) = 0</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        To sketch <m>f'</m>: for each <m>x</m>, plot the slope of <m>f</m>
                        at that point. The resulting curve is <m>y = f'(x)</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <p>
        In <xref ref="sec-average-rates-of-change" text="title"/> we computed the slope of a
        <em>secant line</em> — the straight line through two points on a curve.
        <xref ref="fig-secant-to-tangent"/> shows what happens as the second point <m>Q</m>
        slides toward the fixed point <m>P</m>: the secant line rotates and settles into the
        tangent at <m>P</m>. The slope it settles to is the derivative at <m>P</m> — the
        idea we will make precise with limits in the next section.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-secant-to-tangent">
        <caption>
            As <m>Q</m> approaches <m>P = (1,1)</m> along <m>f(x)=x^2</m>, the secant line
            (grey shades) converges to the tangent at <m>P</m> (red).
        </caption>
        <image width="65%">
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.15]
                    % Axes
                    \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-0.3,0) -- (2.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
                    \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-0.3) -- (0,4.8) node[above] {$y$};
                    \foreach \x in {1,2}
                        \draw (\x,0.07) -- (\x,-0.07) node[below] {\small $\x$};
                    \foreach \y in {1,2,3,4}
                        \draw (0.07,\y) -- (-0.07,\y) node[left] {\small $\y$};
                    % Curve f(x) = x^2
                    \draw[blue, very thick, domain=0:2.1, smooth, samples=60]
                        plot (\x, {\x*\x});
                    \node[blue] at (2.0,4.4) {$f(x)=x^2$};
                    % Fixed point P
                    \filldraw[black] (1,1) circle (2.5pt) node[below right] {$P$};
                    % Secant 1: through Q1=(2,4), slope 3, y = 3x-2
                    \draw[gray!55, domain=0.1:2.3] plot (\x, {3*\x - 2});
                    \filldraw[gray!60] (2,4) circle (2.5pt);
                    \node[gray!70, right] at (2.05,4) {\small $Q_1$};
                    % Secant 2: through Q2=(1.5,2.25), slope 2.5, y = 2.5x-1.5
                    \draw[gray!75, domain=0.2:2.2] plot (\x, {2.5*\x - 1.5});
                    \filldraw[gray!80] (1.5,2.25) circle (2.5pt);
                    \node[gray!80, right] at (1.55,2.25) {\small $Q_2$};
                    % Secant 3: through Q3=(1.2,1.44), slope 2.2, y = 2.2x-1.2
                    \draw[orange!70, domain=0.25:2.15] plot (\x, {2.2*\x - 1.2});
                    \filldraw[orange!80] (1.2,1.44) circle (2.5pt);
                    \node[orange!80, right] at (1.25,1.44) {\small $Q_3$};
                    % Tangent at P: slope 2, y = 2x-1
                    \draw[red, very thick, domain=0.1:2.2] plot (\x, {2*\x - 1});
                    \node[red] at (2.25,3.6) {\small tangent};
                    % Arrow showing Q approaching P
                    \draw[-&gt;, thick, gray!70] (1.55,2.55) -- (1.1,1.25);
                    \node[gray!80, right] at (1.6,2.7) {\scriptsize $Q \to P$};
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
        The graph below shows <m>f(x) = x^3 - 4x</m> with tangent lines drawn at <m>x = -2</m>, <m>x = -1</m>, and <m>x = 2</m>.
        Reading the slopes directly:
        <m>f'(-2) = 8</m>, <m>f'(-1) = -1</m>, <m>f'(2) = 8</m>.
        Notice that the curve is steeply rising at <m>x = -2</m> and <m>x = 2</m> but
        has a small negative slope (gentle fall) at <m>x = -1</m>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-tangents-cubic">
        <caption>
            Tangent lines to <m>f(x) = x^3 - 4x</m> at three points,
            with their slopes labelled.
        </caption>
        <image width="65%">
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
                    \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-2.8,0) -- (2.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
                    \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-4.5) -- (0,4.5) node[above] {$y$};
                    \foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2}
                        \draw (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1) node[below] {\small $\x$};
                    \foreach \y in {-4,-2,2,4}
                        \draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y) node[left] {\small $\y$};
                    % Curve f(x) = x^3 - 4x
                    \draw[blue, very thick, domain=-2.1:2.1, smooth, samples=80]
                        plot (\x, {\x*\x*\x - 4*\x});
                    \node[blue, right] at (1.8,2.0) {$f(x)=x^3-4x$};
                    % Tangent at x=-2: f(-2)=0, slope 8, y=8(x+2)=8x+16
                    % Clip to x in [-2.2, -1.8]
                    \draw[red!70, thick, domain=-2.2:-1.8]
                        plot (\x, {8*\x + 16});
                    \filldraw[red] (-2,0) circle (3pt);
                    \node[red, above] at (-2.75,0.35) {\small $m=8$};
                    % Tangent at x=-1: f(-1)=3, slope -1, y=-x+2
                    \draw[red!70, thick, domain=-1.8:-0.2]
                        plot (\x, {-\x + 2});
                    \filldraw[red] (-1,3) circle (3pt);
                    \node[red, above right] at (-1,3.1) {\small $m=-1$};
                    % Tangent at x=2: f(2)=0, slope 8, y=8(x-2)=8x-16
                    \draw[red!70, thick, domain=1.8:2.2]
                        plot (\x, {8*\x - 16});
                    \filldraw[red] (2,0) circle (3pt);
                    \node[red, above] at (1.55,0.25) {\small $m=8$};
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-sign-of-deriv-cubic">
        <title>Identifying the Sign of the Derivative</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The graph below shows <m>f(x) = x^3 - 3x</m>.
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>State the intervals where <m>f'(x) \gt 0</m>, <m>f'(x) \lt 0</m>,
                        and <m>f'(x) = 0</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Using part (a), sketch a rough graph of <m>f'(x)</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-cubic-f">
                    <caption><m>f(x) = x^3 - 3x</m></caption>
                    <image  width="65%">
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
                                \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-2.2,0) -- (2.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-2.8) -- (0,2.8) node[above] {$y$};
                                \foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2}
                                    \draw (\x,0.08) -- (\x,-0.08) node[below] {\small $\x$};
                                \foreach \y in {-2,-1,1,2}
                                    \draw (0.08,\y) -- (-0.08,\y) node[left] {\small $\y$};
                                \draw[blue, very thick, domain=-1.8:1.8, smooth, samples=70]
                                    plot (\x, {\x*\x*\x - 3*\x});
                                \node[blue, right] at (1.4,1.8) {\small $f(x)$};
                                \filldraw[orange] (-1,2) circle (2.5pt) node[above right] {\scriptsize max};
                                \filldraw[orange] (1,-2) circle (2.5pt) node[below left] {\scriptsize min};
                                \draw[dashed, gray!60] (-1,-2.8) -- (-1,2.8);
                                \draw[dashed, gray!60] (1,-2.8) -- (1,2.8);
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p>Reading the curve in <xref ref="fig-cubic-f"/>:
                        <ul>
                            <li><p><m>f'(x) \gt 0</m> for <m>x \lt -1</m> and <m>x \gt 1</m>
                                (curve rising).</p></li>
                            <li><p><m>f'(x) \lt 0</m> for <m>-1 \lt x \lt 1</m> (curve falling).</p></li>
                            <li><p><m>f'(x) = 0</m> at <m>x = -1</m> and <m>x = 1</m>
                                (horizontal tangents at the turning points).</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                     <p>
                        The derivative <m>f'</m> is zero at <m>x = \pm 1</m>, positive outside, and negative between them — so its graph is a U-shape (a parabola) touching zero at <m>x = -1</m> and <m>x = 1</m>.
                        The figure below shows <m>f'</m>.
                    </p>

                    <figure xml:id="fig-cubic-fprime">
                        <caption><m>f'(x) = 3x^2 - 3</m></caption>
                        <image width="65%">
                            <latex-image>
                                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.95]
                                    \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-2.2,0) -- (2.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                    \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-3.5) -- (0,3.5) node[above] {$y$};
                                    \foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2}
                                        \draw (\x,0.08) -- (\x,-0.08) node[below] {\small $\x$};
                                    \foreach \y in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3}
                                        \draw (0.08,\y) -- (-0.08,\y) node[left] {\scriptsize $\y$};
                                    \draw[red!80, very thick, domain=-1.4:1.4, smooth, samples=60, &lt;-&gt;]
                                        plot (\x, {3*\x*\x - 3});
                                    \node[red!80, right] at (1.5,2.5) {\small $f'(x)$};
                                    \filldraw[orange] (-1,0) circle (2.5pt);
                                    \filldraw[orange] (1,0) circle (2.5pt);
                                    \draw[dashed, gray!60] (-1,-3.5) -- (-1,3.5);
                                    \draw[dashed, gray!60] (1,-3.5) -- (1,3.5);
                                    \node[red!70] at (0,-1.6) {\small $f'\!&lt;\!0$};
                                    \node[green!55!black] at (-1.6,1.2) {\small $f'\!>\!0$};
                                    \node[green!55!black] at (1.6,1.2) {\small $f'\!>\!0$};
                                \end{tikzpicture}
                            </latex-image>
                        </image>
                    </figure>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-reading-tangent-slopes">
        <title>Reading Derivative Values from a Graph</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use <xref ref="fig-tangents-cubic"/> to write down
                <m>f'(-2)</m>, <m>f'(-1)</m>, and <m>f'(2)</m> for
                <m>f(x) = x^3 - 4x</m>.
                What do the values tell you about the curve at each point?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Reading the labelled slopes directly from the diagram:
                <m>f'(-2) = 8</m>, <m>f'(-1) = -1</m>, <m>f'(2) = 8</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                At <m>x = -2</m> the curve is rising steeply (slope 8).
                At <m>x = -1</m> the curve has just passed a local maximum and is falling gently
                (slope <m>-1</m> — negative but small).
                At <m>x = 2</m> the curve is rising steeply again after a local minimum.
                The equal slopes at <m>x = -2</m> and <m>x = 2</m> reflect the odd symmetry
                of <m>f</m> about the origin: the curve rises just as steeply leaving the
                left turning point as it does leaving the right one.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-sketch-deriv-parabola">
        <title>Sketching the Derivative of an Inverted Parabola</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>h(x) = -x^2 + 4</m>.
                Without computing, use the graph of <m>h</m> to sketch <m>h'(x)</m>
                and describe its sign.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Reading the graph of <m>h</m>:
                <ul>
                    <li><p><m>h</m> is increasing for <m>x \lt 0</m>, so <m>h'(x) \gt 0</m> there.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>h</m> has a maximum at <m>x = 0</m>, so <m>h'(0) = 0</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>h</m> is decreasing for <m>x \gt 0</m>, so <m>h'(x) \lt 0</m> there.</p></li>
                </ul>
                <m>h'</m> passes through zero at <m>x = 0</m> and changes from positive to negative —
                it is a decreasing function. Because the parabola is symmetric, <m>h'</m> falls at
                a constant rate: the sketch is a straight line with negative slope through the origin.
            </p>

            <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="auto">
                <figure xml:id="fig-inverted-parabola">
                    <caption><m>h(x) = -x^2 + 4</m></caption>
                    <image>
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.72]
                                \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-2.7,0) -- (2.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-1.2) -- (0,4.8) node[above] {$y$};
                                \foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2}
                                    \draw (\x,0.08) -- (\x,-0.08) node[below] {\small $\x$};
                                \foreach \y in {1,2,3,4}
                                    \draw (0.08,\y) -- (-0.08,\y) node[left] {\small $\y$};
                                \draw[blue, very thick, domain=-2.1:2.1, smooth, samples=60]
                                    plot (\x, {-\x*\x + 4});
                                \node[blue] at (2.0,3.2) {\small $h(x)$};
                                \filldraw[orange] (0,4) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {\scriptsize max};
                                % Horizontal tangent at vertex
                                \draw[orange, thick] (-0.6,4) -- (0.6,4);
                                \node[orange, right] at (0.65,4) {\scriptsize $h'=0$};
                                % Arrows
                                \node[green!55!black] at (-1.5,2.8) {\small $h'\!>\!0$};
                                \node[red!70] at (1.5,2.8) {\small $h'\!&lt;\!0$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <figure xml:id="fig-inverted-parabola-deriv">
                    <caption>Sketch of <m>h'(x)</m></caption>
                    <image>
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.72]
                                \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-2.7,0) -- (2.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-4.8) -- (0,4.8) node[above] {$y$};
                                \foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2}
                                    \draw (\x,0.08) -- (\x,-0.08) node[below] {\small $\x$};
                                \foreach \y in {-4,-2,2,4}
                                    \draw (0.08,\y) -- (-0.08,\y) node[left] {\small $\y$};
                                % h'(x) = -2x
                                \draw[red!80, very thick, domain=-2.2:2.2]
                                    plot (\x, {-2*\x});
                                \node[red!80, left] at (-1.5,3.2) {\small $h'(x)$};
                                \filldraw[red!80] (0,0) circle (2.5pt) node[above right] {\scriptsize $h'(0)=0$};
                                \node[green!55!black] at (-1.3,-1.2) {\small $h'\!>\!0$};
                                \node[red!70] at (1.3,1.2) {\small $h'\!&lt;\!0$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </sidebyside>

            <p>
                The differentiation rules we develop in later sections will confirm
                <m>h'(x) = -2x</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise-sign-simple-parabola">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Sketch the graph of <m>f(x) = x^2 - 1</m>.
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>At which <m>x</m>-value is <m>f'(x) = 0</m>?</p></li>
                        <li><p>State the intervals where <m>f'(x) \gt 0</m> and <m>f'(x) \lt 0</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Sketch a rough graph of <m>f'(x)</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>f</m> has its minimum at <m>x = 0</m>, so <m>f'(0) = 0</m>.
                    <m>f'(x) \lt 0</m> for <m>x \lt 0</m>; <m>f'(x) \gt 0</m> for <m>x \gt 0</m>.
                    The sketch of <m>f'</m> is a line through the origin with positive slope
                    (in fact <m>f'(x) = 2x</m>).
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise-sketch-deriv-cubic">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The graph of a function <m>p</m> is shown below.
                    It has a local maximum at <m>x = -1</m> and a local minimum at <m>x = 2</m>,
                    and is otherwise monotone.
                    Sketch a rough graph of <m>p'(x)</m>, indicating where it is positive,
                    negative, and zero.
                </p>
                <image width="55%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-2.2,0) -- (3.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-2.5) -- (0,3.5) node[above] {$y$};
                            \foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2,3}
                                \draw (\x,0.08) -- (\x,-0.08) node[below] {\small $\x$};
                            \foreach \y in {-2,-1,1,2,3}
                                \draw (0.08,\y) -- (-0.08,\y) node[left] {\small $\y$};
                            % A cubic-like curve with max at (-1,2) and min at (2,-2)
                            % p(x) = x^3/3 - x^2/2 - 2x: local max at x=-1, local min at x=2
                            \draw[blue, very thick, domain=-2.0:3.0, smooth, samples=80]
                                plot (\x, {(1/3)*\x*\x*\x - (1/2)*\x*\x - 2*\x});
                            \node[blue] at (2.9,1.5) {$p(x)$};
                            % Mark turning points
                            \filldraw[orange] (-1,{(1/3)*(-1)*(-1)*(-1) - (1/2)*(-1)*(-1) - 2*(-1)}) circle (2.5pt);
                            \filldraw[orange] (2,{(1/3)*2*2*2 - (1/2)*2*2 - 2*2}) circle (2.5pt);
                            \node[orange, right] at (-0.9,{(1/3)*(-1)^3-(1/2)^2+2.05}) {\scriptsize local max};
                            \node[orange, right] at (2.05,{(1/3)*8-2-4+0.2}) {\scriptsize local min};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>p'(x) \gt 0</m> for <m>x \lt -1</m> and <m>x \gt 2</m>;
                    <m>p'(-1) = 0</m>; <m>p'(x) \lt 0</m> for <m>-1 \lt x \lt 2</m>;
                    <m>p'(2) = 0</m>.
                    The sketch of <m>p'</m> is a U-shaped parabola with zeros at <m>x = -1</m>
                    and <m>x = 2</m>, positive outside those zeros and negative between them.
                    (In fact <m>p'(x) = x^2 - x - 2</m>.)
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise-describe-f-from-fprime-constant">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The graph of <m>f'</m> is a horizontal line at <m>y = 3</m>.
                    Describe the graph of <m>f</m>. Does <m>f</m> have any turning points?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>f'(x) = 3</m> everywhere means <m>f</m> has constant gradient 3 —
                    it is a straight line with slope 3. It has no turning points.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise-reconstruct-f-from-fprime-sign">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A function <m>g</m> satisfies:
                    <m>g'(x) \gt 0</m> for <m>x \lt 2</m>,
                    <m>g'(2) = 0</m>, and
                    <m>g'(x) \lt 0</m> for <m>x \gt 2</m>.
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Describe the behaviour of <m>g</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Does <m>g</m> have a local maximum or local minimum at <m>x = 2</m>?</p></li>
                    <li><p>Sketch a possible graph of <m>g</m>, given also that <m>g(2) = 5</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    (a) <m>g</m> is increasing for <m>x \lt 2</m> and decreasing for <m>x \gt 2</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    (b) Local maximum at <m>x = 2</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    (c) Any smooth curve that rises to a peak of 5 at <m>x = 2</m>
                    and falls on both sides, e.g. an inverted parabola with vertex <m>(2, 5)</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise-min-and-increasing">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A function <m>f</m> satisfies <m>f(0) = 2</m>, <m>f'(0) = 0</m>,
                    <m>f'(x) \lt 0</m> for <m>x \lt 0</m>, and
                    <m>f'(x) \gt 0</m> for <m>x \gt 0</m>.
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Is the point <m>(0, 2)</m> a local maximum or local minimum of <m>f</m>?</p></li>
                    <li><p>Sketch a possible graph of <m>f</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    (a) <m>f</m> is decreasing for <m>x \lt 0</m> and increasing for <m>x \gt 0</m>,
                    so <m>f</m> has a local <em>minimum</em> at <m>x = 0</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    (b) Any upward-opening curve with a minimum at <m>(0, 2)</m>,
                    e.g. a parabola <m>f(x) = x^2 + 2</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise-aroc-approx-deriv">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The table below gives values of a function <m>f</m>.
                </p>
                <table>
                    <title>Values of <m>f</m></title>
                    <tabular halign="center">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                            <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                            <cell>0</cell>
                            <cell>1</cell>
                            <cell>2</cell>
                            <cell>3</cell>
                            <cell>4</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
                            <cell>1</cell>
                            <cell>3</cell>
                            <cell>7</cell>
                            <cell>13</cell>
                            <cell>21</cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Estimate <m>f'(1)</m>, <m>f'(2)</m>, and <m>f'(3)</m> by computing
                            the AROC over the interval on each side of the point and averaging:
                            <md>f'(a) \approx \frac{f(a+1) - f(a-1)}{2}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            What pattern do you see in your estimates?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f'(1) \approx \tfrac{f(2)-f(0)}{2} = \tfrac{7-1}{2} = 3</m>;
                            <m>f'(2) \approx \tfrac{f(3)-f(1)}{2} = \tfrac{13-3}{2} = 5</m>;
                            <m>f'(3) \approx \tfrac{f(4)-f(2)}{2} = \tfrac{21-7}{2} = 7</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The estimates <m>3, 5, 7</m> are consecutive odd numbers — they increase by 2 each time, suggesting <m>f'(x) = 2x + 1</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </solution>
        </exercise>


    </exercises>

